# Pinned differential cases — known, DOCUMENTED divergences from the oracle.
#
#   CASE_ID   CORPUS   SECTION   reason
#
# CASE_ID and CORPUS are shell GLOBS, so `callees-*` pins a whole command
# family and a bare `*` still means everything.  SECTION names the entry in
# dev/DIVERGENCES.md that explains the case; a pin with no such entry is not a
# pin, it is an unexplained failure with the alarm switched off.
#
# A glob must not over-reach: a pinned case that AGREES is a stale pin, so
# `unused-*` may pin the family only because EVERY member of it differs here.
#
# A pinned case that AGREES is reported as a STALE PIN and fails the run: a pin
# that no longer describes anything is a lie about the state of the port.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D7 and D10 were RESOLVED UPSTREAM in 0.8.0, and their pins are gone with them
#
# D7 was the oracle's `_build_line_index` raising TypeError on a multi-name
# `axiomatization` (it sorted `(src_start, thy_end, Entry)` and fell through to
# comparing two dataclasses), which made `grep`, `sorry` and the whole usage
# family a traceback on FOL and ZF -- 132 pinned cases across the two.  D10 was
# `unused -r`'s cascade depth depending on the interpreter's hash seed, because
# the loop re-read the `unused` set its own body was growing.  Upstream fixed
# both (`[span-ties]`, `[cascade-level]`), so at oracle 0.8.1 every one of those
# cases AGREES -- and a pin that describes nothing is a stale pin, which fails
# the run by design.  They are deleted rather than reworded.
#
# The evidence is not deleted: `dev/DIVERGENCES.md` keeps D7 and D10 with the
# measurements that established them, marked with the release that closed them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D8 — the oracle exits 120, not 141, when the broken pipe is only noticed by
# the interpreter's shutdown flush.  Corpus-dependent: 120 on the smallest
# corpus, 141 on the other four, for the same invocation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

closed-stdout   Abstract_Completeness   D8   oracle exits 120 (shutdown-flush BrokenPipeError)

# The same defect from the other side: when the WHOLE answer fits in the 64K
# pipe buffer the oracle's writes never fail at all, so it exits 0 for a
# `census | head -3` that the rewrite reports as 141.  Deterministic in the
# output size, not a race: exactly the two corpora whose census is under 64K
# (Abstract_Completeness 34K, CTT 54K); the other five are 141 on both sides.
shape-census-pipe   Abstract_Completeness   D8   whole census fits the pipe buffer; oracle exits 0
shape-census-pipe   CTT                     D8   whole census fits the pipe buffer; oracle exits 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D8 — the demo corpora sit between the two thresholds
#
# Both demo sessions are a few hundred lines, so their answers are small: the
# oracle fills a 64K pipe buffer without a single write failing and exits 0,
# while this engine's writer encodes 8K at a time, so an answer over 8K leaves
# in several writes and the second one meets a `head` that has already gone --
# 141.  Between 8K and 64K the two therefore disagree, which is every pin here:
# `Demo_Core` `find ... -a -V` and its census, and `Demo_Extras` `find ... -a
# -V` at 12,739 bytes.  D8's own tables name both thresholds.
#
# `Demo_Extras` used to differ in ~60 cases as well, because it holds the
# `definition\<^marker>\<open>tag important\<close> marked_const` showcase that the
# oracle's DECL_RE could not match (D2) -- and pinning THOSE would have hidden
# the showcase.  Upstream fixed its side in 0.8.0, so they are clean now and
# there is nothing left to hide; only the pipe case remains.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

closed-stdout          Demo_Core     D8   answer fits the pipe buffer: oracle exits 0
shape-census-pipe      Demo_Core     D8   answer fits the pipe buffer: oracle exits 0
closed-stdout          Demo_Extras   D8   12,739 bytes: over our writer's 8K, under the 64K pipe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D2 — the demo tree is ONE project, and it contains the marker showcase
#
# `demo/.isabelle-query` makes both session directories resolve to `demo`, so
# that the jEdit walkthrough can query across them (the locale is declared in
# Demo_Core and instantiated in Demo_Extras).  A marker beats -R nothing and
# ROOT proximity, so the two cwd-discovery cases read the WHOLE tree -- which
# includes the `\<^marker>` showcase.  That USED to make them the one place a
# Demo_Core run differed, and it is why `demo/` was built the way it is.
#
# The pin is GONE as of P9: `[marker-decl]` closed D2 on both sides, so the
# case agrees and a pin on it is a stale pin, which fails the run by design.
# The comment stays because the fixture was designed around it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
